input.explanation:
The bad port in values-production.yaml is what broke, but the reason a wrong endpoint turned into a 7x latency regression lives in this repository: `cached_summary` dials the cache once per account-page render and remembers nothing about what happened last time. With the endpoint refusing connections, all 1200 requests/min each paid a full connect-and-fail before falling back, so an optimisation that is supposed to cost nothing when absent became a fixed tax on every request. The fallback worked exactly as designed - every page was correct - which is precisely why this showed up only in p50/p95 and cache_hit_ratio and not in the error rate.

The fix gives the module a memory, per endpoint. After `FAILURES_BEFORE_PAUSING` (3) consecutive unreachable answers the endpoint is not dialled again for `PAUSE_SECONDS` (30). `CacheUnreachable` is still raised on every such request, still naming the host and port, now saying plainly that the shop did not dial and when it next will. So:

- the failure is never silenced - `RenderedPage.cache_failure` is still set on every request and the log line still carries `cache.io-shop.svc.cluster.local:6380`, so the diagnosis (logged endpoint vs. configured endpoint) is unchanged;
- the cost is bounded to one probe per 30s per endpoint rather than one per request, so a misconfigured or genuinely down cache is the slowdown the fallback intends rather than a latency incident;
- recovery is automatic and needs no deploy: the first answer of any kind clears the count and the pause, so a port put back to 6379 is in use again within 30 seconds;
- one endpoint's refusals do not affect another, since the memory is keyed by address.

The clock is a seam (`now`, defaulting to `time.monotonic`) so the pause is testable without sleeping, and `forget_cache_failures()` gives tests - and a deployment that wants an immediate retry - a reset. `cached_summary`'s existing signature and behaviour are otherwise unchanged, so `account_page._the_figure_for`, the only caller, needed no edit and behaves identically in both states (cache configured and not).

The new tests fail against the code as it stands: `test_a_cache_that_keeps_refusing_is_not_dialled_on_every_request` counts actual dials over 200 requests against an always-refusing cache and asserts they stop at 3 - today it is 200, one per request, which is the incident measured directly. `test_a_paused_cache_still_reports_the_endpoint_it_cannot_reach` pins that not dialling does not cost the diagnosis, and `test_a_cache_that_comes_back_is_used_again` pins recovery. Both test files get an autouse reset so the new module state cannot leak between cases.
